.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"The Data Link Layer's Role in Reliable Data Transfer\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Data Link Layer, positioned as the second layer in the OSI Model, is paramount for ensuring reliable data transfer across a physical medium or link [Data: Entities (1178); Relationships (2038)]. This layer is responsible for managing access to the communication medium, a critical function particularly in local network environments. To fulfill its duties, the Data Link Layer is divided into two sublayers: the Logical Link Control (LLC) and the Medium Access Control (MAC) sublayers. The LLC sublayer interfaces with the network layer above it, managing error and flow control for the data link, while the MAC sublayer dictates how devices access the physical medium, preventing collisions and ensuring orderly data transmission [Data: Entities (1178)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Sublayers of the Data Link Layer: LLC and MAC\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Data Link Layer is comprised of two distinct sublayers: Logical Link Control (LLC) and Medium Access Control (MAC). The LLC sublayer is responsible for providing an interface to the network layer and handles error and flow control, optionally supporting multiple network protocols [Data: Entities (1178), (1522)]. It is particularly useful for interoperability between different network protocols at the link layer and in networks that require robust flow control. The MAC sublayer, on the other hand, governs how devices gain access to the physical network medium, arbitrating access to prevent data collisions and ensure efficient transmission [Data: Entities (1178)]. The relationship between LLC and the Data Link Layer highlights its function as the upper sublayer responsible for protocol multiplexing and control [Data: Relationships (2039)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Interrelation of OSI Layers\",\n            \"explanatio